Two thoughts before the code.

Templates: yes, but as skeletons with register guidance, not fill-in-the-blank forms. The risk with rigid templates is that every tutorial ends up with the same seven sections whether or not that's the right shape. What they should constrain is register — the thing an AI most reliably gets wrong. A concept page written in imperative voice is worse than one with an unusual heading structure.

Styling around templates: only via frontmatter, not content position. Rules like "the first paragraph is a lede" break the moment someone opens with a note. Frontmatter is declarative and robust — and it lets you render a real metadata strip instead of CSS-injected text, which is better for screen readers anyway.

So I've replaced the CSS ::before section label with a proper metadata bar driven by frontmatter. That's the piece that ties templates and styling together.

