AGENTS.md — documentation section
markdown
## Documentation

Pages are organized by what the reader needs, following Diátaxis. A page
belongs to exactly one kind and does not mix them.

| Kind | Directory | Reader wants | Register |
|---|---|---|---|
| Tutorial | `docs/start/` | first success | we, step by step, one path |
| How-to | `docs/guides/` | to complete a task | imperative, assumes context |
| Reference | `docs/reference/` | a specific fact | terse, complete, neutral |
| Explanation | `docs/concepts/` | to understand | discursive, takes positions |
| Background | `docs/background/` | prerequisite knowledge | short primers, links to depth |

Start from the matching template in `templates/docs/`. The comment block at
the top states the rules for that kind — read it, then delete it.

When writing a page, the most common failure is mixing kinds. If a how-to
starts explaining why the design is what it is, that paragraph belongs in
`concepts/` and the guide should link to it instead.

Every command shown in documentation must have been run. Every output shown
must be real. Do not invent paths, durations, seeds, or error messages.
Where a real file is being quoted, use a snippet so it cannot drift:

```toml
    --8<-- "../blocks/exemplar/ingot.toml"
```

Frontmatter is optional. `time` and `needs` render as a metadata strip;
`status: draft` or `status: stub` renders a badge. Use `status` on any
page that is not finished.
The status badge is the piece I'd use immediately

Every stub page you create gets status: stub in its frontmatter, and it renders a red pill in the metadata bar. That's honest with readers, visible to you when browsing, and greppable:

bash
grep -rl "status: stub" docs/

Your writing queue, maintained by the pages themselves.
